            text: 'A university debating final, a few years ago, was decided not by the team with the more dramatic rhetoric but by the one that had quietly built its case on a single, carefully substantiated premise, leaving its opponents to attack a position they had never actually occupied. That mismatch, a brilliant delivery resting on a shaky foundation, is more common than most people assume, and it explains why arguments that sound compelling in the moment frequently collapse under closer scrutiny the next morning. What underpins any serious argument is not confidence of tone but the strength of the premise it stands on, since even flawless logic will carry a false conclusion if the starting assumption is wrong. Consider a familiar example: for decades, a tenet of personal finance advice held that renting a home was simply money thrown away compared with buying one. It is precisely this unexamined tenet that a wave of younger economists has spent the last fifteen years quietly dismantling, pointing out that the comparison depends enormously on local interest rates, opportunity cost and how long someone actually intends to stay in one place. None of this means renting is always wiser; it means the original claim was stated with far more certainty than the underlying evidence ever supported. Good argument-construction begins, in practice, with stating one’s premise so plainly that an opponent could, if they wished, attack it directly rather than attacking a caricature of it. This is harder than it sounds, because a vague premise is difficult to discredit and therefore feels, falsely, safer to defend. What matters most, then, is not merely assembling evidence but arranging it so that each piece visibly supports the premise rather than merely accompanying it; a long list of facts is not, by itself, an argument. Lawyers are trained precisely for this discipline: a closing statement that lists forty facts without ever stating what they are meant to prove will persuade almost no one, however factually accurate it remains. There is a reasonable objection here worth taking seriously: some fields, particularly the sciences, progress through evidence that resists tidy premises, accumulating weight gradually rather than snapping into a single clean argument. That is a fair qualification, yet it does not undercut the broader point; even cumulative, probabilistic evidence eventually needs to be organised around some claim precise enough to be tested and, if necessary, discredited. All that a well-built argument ultimately promises is clarity about what would have to be true for the conclusion to follow; it cannot, on its own, guarantee that the conclusion is correct. What separates a durable argument from a merely persuasive one is exactly this willingness to expose its own premise to challenge, rather than wrapping it in language too vague to be tested at all.',
