            text: 'During a televised debate a few years ago, a candidate facing a detailed question about pension funding replied instead with a vivid story about a single struggling pensioner, and the studio audience applauded as though the question had actually been answered. It had not; the anecdote was a classic red herring, emotionally effective and almost entirely irrelevant to whether the underlying numbers added up. Political rhetoric notwithstanding, the arithmetic of a pension system does not change because a story about one person moved a room full of voters. This is not a trivial sleight of hand; it is one of the oldest and most durable fallacies in public argument, the substitution of a sympathetic particular case for an unanswered general question. A demagogue, in the classical sense, is simply someone who has learned to exploit this substitution reliably, offering audiences the emotional satisfaction of resolution without the substance of it. Consider the spurious correlation occasionally paraded in newspaper columns between, say, falling crime rates and a politician’s preferred policy, when crime had in fact been falling for years before that policy existed and continued falling in neighbouring regions that never adopted it. Albeit superficially persuasive, such correlations collapse the moment anyone checks the timeline, yet they circulate for years because checking takes longer than repeating. Some defenders of such rhetoric argue, not unreasonably, that political communication cannot function purely through spreadsheets, and that stories and feeling have a legitimate place in public persuasion. Be that as it may, there remains a meaningful distinction between illustrating a true conclusion with a vivid example and substituting the example for the conclusion itself; the first is good rhetoric, the second is a fallacy wearing rhetoric’s clothes. Straw-manning, the practice of attacking a simplified, weaker version of an opponent’s actual position, thrives for the same reason: it is far easier to discredit a caricature than to engage with the strongest form of an argument. Ad hominem attacks on a speaker’s character, rather than their claim, persist notwithstanding centuries of logic textbooks explaining precisely why such attacks prove nothing about the claim itself. What makes these fallacies dangerous is not that they are irrational on their face, since humans have always responded more readily to story than to statistic, but that they are reliably effective regardless of whether the underlying position is sound. A rhetorically skilled demagogue and a rhetorically skilled statesman can deploy identical techniques; what distinguishes them, in the end, is whether the conclusion their audience is nudged toward happens to be true. Recognising a fallacy, then, is less about distrusting emotion altogether and more about asking, every time a story moves you, exactly what claim it is quietly being used to settle.',
